question:
Referring to the B2025 Load Realization Model, please provide a spreadsheet which includes all large load projects introduced to the Company’s pipeline since the filing provided in the 2023 IRP Update. For each project, provide the information outlined below. If any information is unavailable, please explain why and provide the remaining information as requested in spreadsheet format with any formulas in tact.
a. 	Project Name;
b. 	Class;
c. 	Segment;
d. 	Location relative to the Company’s service territory (internal/external);
e. 	Prob 1, 2, 3, and Prob Product;
f. 	Announced Load, as most recently accurate (MW);
g. 	Expected annual energy demand (GWh);
h. 	Expected annual load factor (%);
i. 	Quarterly Load Ramps, as most recently accurate (MW);
j. 	Annual Load Ramps, as most recently accurate (MW);
k. 	Cumulative changes to announced load since the initial announcement (MW);
l. 	Cumulative delays to commercial operations since the initial announcement (months);
m. 	Date at which the project entered the Company’s pipeline;
n. 	Project Status (Technical Review, Request for Electric Service, Contract for Electric Service, Cancelled);
o. 	Date at which the project was cancelled or otherwise removed, if applicable; and
p. 	Construction Status (Pending Construction, Broken Ground, Commercially Operating).

Please see page 147 of the IRP document, where benefits of Georgia Power participating in the Southern Company Pool are identified. Two of the benefits are described as follows:
Carrying a lower generation planning reserve margin (due primarily to System load diversity), which enables each Operating Company to have a lower investment in generating resources;
Enhanced reliability of electric service through the use of transmission interconnections to provide backup service in case of emergencies as well as providing the ability to import lower cost energy when available;
Questions:
In calculating the system and individual utility planning reserve margins, is there a deliverability component to ensure that resources owned by one operating company are deliverable to the other operating company?
To what extent does the system do joint transmission planning among Georgia Power, Alabama Power, and Mississippi Power to maximize the benefit of enhanced reliability through use of the transmission system?

response:
Georgia Power’s annual expenditures on cybersecurity programs have varied over the five-year period, reflecting both changes in accounting treatment and a strategic expansion of cybersecurity initiatives. Overall, expenditures increased both in total and as a share of the IT budget (from 2–7 percent in early years to approximately 8 percent in 2025). These increases reflect the Company’s ongoing commitment to maintaining robust cybersecurity capabilities and ensuring alignment with industry best practices and regulatory expectations.
The increase in cybersecurity spending beginning in 2024 is primarily associated with the expansion of cybersecurity activities across the enterprise. Contributing factors include:
Broader implementation of cybersecurity technologies such as network segmentation, advanced monitoring, and system hardening;
Growth in cybersecurity staffing to support continuous operations and new platforms;
Use of third-party service providers for assessments and managed security support; and
Additional resources devoted to regulatory compliance and employee awareness programs.

Georgia Power forecasts cybersecurity expenditures as part of its broader information technology and corporate risk management planning process. Forecasts are developed annually and refined through the Company’s multi-year budgeting cycle in coordination with Southern Company Services.
Projected spending levels are informed by:
Expected technology lifecycle upgrades and system modernization needs;
Anticipated regulatory and compliance requirements;
Industry benchmarks and evolving best practices;
Enterprise risk assessments that consider emerging cyber threats and operational resilience objectives; and
Workforce planning to maintain appropriate security staffing and expertise.
Cybersecurity budgets are reviewed and adjusted periodically to reflect changes in threat environment, business operations, and regulatory guidance. Forecasts are not based on specific threat events but on prudent planning assumptions consistent with maintaining a robust and adaptive cybersecurity posture.

Yes. Correspondence varies based on the subject matter and applicable business unit and may occur via phone call, virtual meeting, or in-person meeting. For meetings, the Company typically retains calendar invitation records, which include the meeting agenda and list of attendees.
Informal communication logs related to cyber threat intelligence are retained using various applications and mediums. They are appropriately labeled using the Traffic Light Protocol (“TLP”) for cyber threat information sharing. These trade secrets are protected under bilateral and multilateral non-disclosure agreements, carrying the TLP marking of Amber+Strict, which prohibits external disclosure.
The retained information is maintained in forms appropriate to the nature of the communication and is used to support the Company’s physical and cyber security operations. This includes:
maintaining situational awareness;
coordinating and sharing information with internal stakeholders and applicable external agencies;
aligning roles and responsibilities in response to evolving conditions affecting the electric sector and critical infrastructure environment;
assessing, adjusting, and validating the Company’s physical security posture;
proactively identifying previously unknown cyber threats within Company data;
establishing an internal knowledge base of threat actor techniques; and
modeling real-world threats during tabletop exercises.
